1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,264
Every booking starts as a message. This is the real console, on the
live service.

2
00:00:03,264 --> 00:00:12,960
Anna gives everything in one go — two guests, three nights from
October seventeenth, one room, both of them diving, and a ride from
the airport.

3
00:00:12,960 --> 00:00:14,832
Watch what comes back.

4
00:00:14,832 --> 00:00:23,208
One message in, one complete enquiry out. Every detail read back,
and a person still confirms the price.

5
00:00:52,860 --> 00:00:53,460
Same assistant, a different shape of booking.

6
00:00:54,312 --> 00:00:54,912
Lucia writes for three people — two rooms, full board, and only two
of them dive. She names the days she wants to dive, and asks for the
airport pickup.

7
00:01:04,872 --> 00:01:05,472
Three guests, two divers. Those are different numbers, and the
system keeps them apart.

8
00:01:11,065 --> 00:01:11,665
Nothing to chase and nothing invented. The diving is priced from two
people, not three.

9
00:01:44,380 --> 00:01:44,980
Now the hard one. A dive club books for a group.

10
00:01:46,288 --> 00:01:46,888
Marco needs five twin rooms for ten guests, four nights, full board,
and airport transfer from Manila.

11
00:01:53,440 --> 00:01:54,040
Six fields from one message. All it asks back is about the diving.

12
00:01:58,408 --> 00:01:59,008
The group does not dive on the same days.

13
00:02:01,168 --> 00:02:01,768
Six dive for four days, the other four dive for two — and he asks
about their partner rate.

14
00:02:07,984 --> 00:02:08,584
No single number describes that, so the assistant keeps his own
words as the schedule instead of inventing one.

15
00:02:15,232 --> 00:02:15,832
The partner rate is routed to your team, never quoted by the
assistant. That is the whole idea: the conversation is instant, and
every decision about money stays with your people.
